Here's the pattern we use in PayStream's settlement service (see `internal/settlement/writer.go`): use a ticker-based flush with a size trigger.

go
const batchSize = 100
const flushInterval = 50 * time.Millisecond

ticker := time.NewTicker(flushInterval)
var pending []*pb.Transaction

for {
  select {
  case tx := <-txnChan:
    pending = append(pending, tx)
    if len(pending) >= batchSize {
      flush(pending) // single InsertBatch call
      pending = nil
    }
  case <-ticker.C:
    if len(pending) > 0 {
      flush(pending)
      pending = nil
    }
  }
}


This bounds memory to 100 transactions max and keeps database batching. Latency impact: worst case 50ms added latency for low-volume txns, but your P99 at 500 TPS stays tight because you're always flushing in batches.

