Ah, this is the classic Context re-render problem. When any value in your Context Provider changes, all consumers re-render — it's a broadcast pattern. You're seeing this because you probably have something like:

javascript
// TaskFiltersContext.jsx - DON'T do this
const [filters, setFilters] = useState(...);
const value = { filters, setFilters, sortBy, setSortBy, ... }; // too many things
<TaskFiltersContext.Provider value={value}>


Every time `setFilters` is called, a new `value` object is created, so all children re-render.

**Quick fix: Split your Context.** Create separate contexts for things that update at different frequencies:

javascript
// TaskFiltersContext.jsx
const TaskFiltersContext = createContext();
export const useTaskFilters = () => useContext(TaskFiltersContext);

// TaskListContext.jsx
const TaskListContext = createContext();
export const useTaskList = () => useContext(TaskListContext);


Then TaskCard only subscribes to `useTaskList` (which contains static task data), while FilterPanel subscribes to `useTaskFilters`. Changes to filters won't cause TaskCard to re-render.

**Better fix: Switch to Zustand.** Zustand has built-in selector optimization — you only re-render if the *specific slice* you selected changes:

javascript
// src/store/taskStore.js
import create from 'zustand';

export const useTaskStore = create((set) => ({
  filters: { status: 'all', assignee: null },
  setFilters: (newFilters) => set((state) => ({ filters: newFilters })),
  tasks: [],
  setTasks: (tasks) => set({ tasks }),
}));

// In TaskCard.jsx
const tasks = useTaskStore((state) => state.tasks); // only re-renders if tasks change
const filters = useTaskStore((state) => state.filters); // separate subscription


Which direction feels better for your team — splitting Context or trying Zustand?

