const tasks = useTaskStore((state) => state.tasks);
useEffect(() => {
  fetchTasks(filters);
}, [filters]);


Why? Because your FastAPI backend is the source of truth. The hook handles the API contract and side effects; the store handles client-side state. Keeps concerns separated. If you needed to retry logic or debouncing, it'd live in the hook. If you needed computed state (like `tasksGroupedByAssignee`), that lives in the store.

