Great questions — you're thinking about this right:

**Dependency loop:** You won't get infinite renders if you're careful. The key is *not* putting the entire selector object in the dependency array. Do this:

javascript
// BAD - filters object changes reference every time
const filters = useTaskStore((state) => state.filters);
useEffect(() => {
  fetchTasks(filters);
}, [filters]); // This re-runs every render

// GOOD - only re-run if the filter values actually changed
useEffect(() => {
  const unsubscribe = useTaskStore.subscribe(
    (state) => state.filters,
    (filters) => fetchTasks(filters)
  );
  return unsubscribe;
}, []); // Empty array - Zustand manages subscription


Or even simpler, use Zustand's shallow comparison:

javascript
import { shallow } from 'zustand/react/shallow';

const filters = useTaskStore(
  (state) => state.filters,
  shallow // only re-render if filter values differ
);

useEffect(() => {
  fetchTasks(filters);
}, [filters]); // Safe now


