The Prisma middleware hook is the cleaner approach. In your prisma/schema.prisma, you can't directly add middleware, but you can wrap the client instantiation in lib/db/prisma-client.ts.

Here's the pattern:

typescript
// lib/db/prisma-client.ts
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

const globalForPrisma = global as unknown as { prisma: PrismaClient };

export const prisma = globalForPrisma.prisma || new PrismaClient();

if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') globalForPrisma.prisma = prisma;

// Add middleware to set tenant context
prisma.$use(async (params, next) => {
  // params.args contains the actual query arguments
  const tenantId = params.args._tenantId; // passed via context

  if (!tenantId) {
    throw new Error('No tenant context set for query');
  }

  // Set the tenant context at the connection level
  await prisma.$executeRawUnsafe(`SET app.current_tenant_id = $1::uuid`, [tenantId]);

  return next(params);
});


But wait—this has a problem: you can't pass `_tenantId` through Prisma's query builder. The middleware approach doesn't solve your injection problem cleanly.

**Better solution: Use Prisma's `$executeRaw()` with a transaction wrapper:**

typescript
// lib/db/tenant-db.ts
export async function executeWithTenantContext<T>(
  tenantId: string,
  callback: (client: PrismaClient) => Promise<T>
): Promise<T> {
  return await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
    await tx.$executeRawUnsafe(`SET app.current_tenant_id = '${tenantId}'`);
    return callback(tx);
  });
}


Then in your API routes:
typescript
// pages/api/dashboards.ts
await executeWithTenantContext(tenantId, async (tx) => {
  return tx.dashboard.findMany(); // RLS is automatically applied
});


This ensures every query in the transaction inherits the tenant context. The transaction boundary is key—RLS policies are evaluated per-statement, and the SET statement lives in the same transaction.

